WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.870
Today, I want to talk about learning
related or similar languages.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:08.309
Is it an advantage to learn a
language that's similar to one

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:12.000
that you already know, and are
there some problems with that?

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:16.830
First of all, as we learn a new
language, of course, we relate

00:00:16.859 --> 00:00:20.269
everything new to something that we know.

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:23.590
If we just take the language full of
words and sounds we've never heard

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:26.940
before, and we can't relate it to
something that we know, something that

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:29.649
we're familiar with, we don't have that
something we're not going to learn.

00:00:29.649 --> 00:00:32.399
So we relate things back to
things that we already know.

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:38.879
And as we progress in the language and
we see new words that are different forms

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.230
or changed forms of words that we already
know, the fact that we see these words

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.735
that Are familiar to what we already know.

00:00:45.915 --> 00:00:47.295
That helps us learn.

00:00:47.585 --> 00:00:51.785
And of course we gain confidence as
we continue learning the language

00:00:51.845 --> 00:00:55.445
and then we push the envelope into
things that we don't know, and they

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.875
eventually become things that we know.

00:00:58.175 --> 00:01:02.975
And if we keep going, we will inevitably
improve as we learn a language.

00:01:03.065 --> 00:01:04.739
So that being the case.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.820
If we learn a similar language,
that should be an advantage.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.020
And of course it is.

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:14.630
Because the biggest sort of task in
language learning is to acquire words.

00:01:14.899 --> 00:01:21.409
Well, if 50, 60, 70 percent of the
words of a new language are similar or

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.180
identifiable, that should make it easier.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.010
French, for example, half of
the vocabulary is identifiable

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:29.750
from English, or vice versa.

00:01:29.995 --> 00:01:34.175
So those languages, for that reason,
are easier to learn, say for an

00:01:34.175 --> 00:01:37.655
English speaker to learn French
as opposed to Greek or Chinese.

00:01:37.765 --> 00:01:40.295
So that is a big advantage.

00:01:40.545 --> 00:01:42.005
And there are many examples.

00:01:42.005 --> 00:01:43.454
It's not just English and French.

00:01:43.615 --> 00:01:45.455
It's obviously the Romance languages.

00:01:45.455 --> 00:01:48.914
If you know Spanish, it's easier
to learn Italian or Portuguese.

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:52.664
If you know Russian, it's easier to
learn Czech or Ukrainian or Polish.

00:01:52.704 --> 00:01:56.991
And even encountering this in the case
of Arabic, where having started with,

00:01:56.991 --> 00:01:59.285
with Fusha, which is standard Arabic.

00:01:59.755 --> 00:02:04.485
I now take all of that vocabulary
and familiarity with how the Arabic

00:02:04.485 --> 00:02:08.475
language works in a way and apply
that to say Levantine Arabic, which

00:02:08.484 --> 00:02:10.234
is in many ways quite different.

00:02:10.774 --> 00:02:14.464
However, what I want to point out
is that there are some pitfalls.

00:02:14.475 --> 00:02:15.864
There are some difficulties.

00:02:15.924 --> 00:02:19.665
First of all, when we learn a
language and then start to speak in

00:02:19.665 --> 00:02:23.375
a language, there are sort of three
things that happen in our brain as we.

00:02:23.725 --> 00:02:25.095
Try to produce an utterance.

00:02:25.375 --> 00:02:28.195
First of all, there is a word that
will come to mind in the language.

00:02:28.215 --> 00:02:30.435
And we'll throw that out there
because we're comfortable using it.

00:02:30.555 --> 00:02:34.225
And apparently, we then go to a
structure that we have used before.

00:02:34.414 --> 00:02:36.975
So, presumably, at some point,
we threw a structure out there.

00:02:36.984 --> 00:02:39.294
It worked, so we want to
use that structure again.

00:02:39.345 --> 00:02:43.065
But another important part of
what the brain does, it suppresses

00:02:43.135 --> 00:02:44.405
things that don't belong.

00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:47.435
And that's an important part
of, you know, developing that

00:02:47.435 --> 00:02:49.174
ability to speak in a language.

00:02:49.204 --> 00:02:50.355
So what happens?

00:02:50.644 --> 00:02:55.605
If the languages are very closely related,
at least in my experience, I find it

00:02:55.615 --> 00:03:01.345
more difficult to suppress those things
from related languages that don't belong.

00:03:01.425 --> 00:03:05.015
When I speak Portuguese, for example,
I'm accused of speaking Portuñol.

00:03:05.015 --> 00:03:14.011
I started with the Spanish language, so it's difficult for me to create

00:03:14.011 --> 00:03:18.209
 the neuronal connections in the brain.

00:03:18.845 --> 00:03:21.535
to speak better in Portuguese

00:03:21.535 --> 00:03:25.668
because I have this influence from Spanish.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:26.830
There's a lot of Spanish.

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.680
Spanish pronunciation.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:33.650
Spanish phraseology or structure,
Spanish vocabulary, even some Italian.

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:34.490
So those things.

00:03:34.905 --> 00:03:36.885
tend to kind of get a bit mixed.

00:03:36.895 --> 00:03:40.925
Now, hopefully if I were in
a purely Portuguese speaking

00:03:40.925 --> 00:03:46.435
environment, then I would speak
more Portuguese and less Portugal.

00:03:46.605 --> 00:03:50.124
Similarly with these other pairs, these
other like Slavic language groups.

00:03:50.215 --> 00:03:53.595
And similarly with standard
Arabic and say Levantine Arabic,

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:56.185
it doesn't hamper comprehension.

00:03:56.335 --> 00:04:00.924
And if we remember that comprehension
is the sort of core skill upon which

00:04:00.945 --> 00:04:06.325
all other skills are built, then If you
understand well and you still confuse

00:04:06.325 --> 00:04:07.925
them a little bit, it's not a big deal.

00:04:08.165 --> 00:04:13.904
Another example of this learning similar
languages is the example of Romanian.

00:04:14.125 --> 00:04:16.655
Romanian for me was very easy.

00:04:16.695 --> 00:04:21.044
I had the advantage that it had
say 70 percent vocabulary that was

00:04:21.044 --> 00:04:25.845
similar to Romance languages, another
20 percent is apparently of Slavic

00:04:25.845 --> 00:04:28.705
or of some Balkan area languages.

00:04:29.075 --> 00:04:31.524
But 70 percent is from the Romance base.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:33.680
But the structure is quite different.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:38.390
And so therefore it was easier for me
to suppress things that didn't belong.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:44.400
So blessings and disadvantages, primarily
it's a blessing, the sort of determining

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:50.570
factor of how easy a language to learn
still remains how similar the languages

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:54.860
are and how similar the vocabulary
is, but you can't take it for granted.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.220
And it's very, sometimes very difficult.

00:04:57.615 --> 00:05:01.535
Once you have acquired, let's say in my
case, Spanish, and I put myself into a

00:05:01.535 --> 00:05:06.045
Spanish frame of mind, and I've learned
to imitate Spanish, it's almost like

00:05:06.045 --> 00:05:09.945
I'm an actor playing that role, and now
I'm asked to play this other role, this

00:05:09.945 --> 00:05:11.915
Portuguese role, it's difficult to do.

00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:13.994
So comprehension is good.

00:05:14.475 --> 00:05:17.775
Getting to where you speak that
other language well is something

00:05:17.775 --> 00:05:19.455
that I have found difficult to do.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.490
Other people, I know a lot of excellent
polyglots who are sounding close to

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.710
native in related languages, so it
may just be a problem that I have,

00:05:30.009 --> 00:05:34.159
but I thought it would be interesting
to share this with you and, uh,

00:05:34.249 --> 00:05:35.859
I look forward to your comments.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:36.840
Bye.

